---
/**
 * The full-bleed hero for article and case-study pages.
 *
 * ── THE PANORAMA IS A CONSEQUENCE, NOT A DECLARATION ─────────────────────────
 *
 * The brief reads as two designs — 16:9 on a phone, panoramic on a desktop — and
 * it is one, expressed in three properties:
 *
 *     inline-size: 100%      full bleed at every width
 *     aspect-ratio: 16/9     the shape while there is room for it
 *     max-block-size: 24rem  the height it is not allowed to exceed
 *
 * Below 682.7px the ratio governs. Above it the height pins at 384px while the
 * width keeps growing, so the box widens into 2.08:1 at 800, 3.75:1 at 1440 and
 * 5:1 at 1920 — the panorama, arrived at rather than specified. Measured across
 * all of those widths.
 *
 * THE SWITCH POINT IS DERIVED, which is the reason this needs no breakpoint and
 * could not sensibly use one: 682.7 is wherever 16:9 happens to meet 24rem, so it
 * moves if either constant does. A media query would have to be recomputed by hand
 * every time; `max-block-size` simply cannot be wrong.
 *
 * `object-fit: cover` fills the widening box, and `object-position` aims the crop
 * — see the hotspot note below.
 *
 * ── WHAT THIS COSTS, AND WHY IT IS ACCEPTED ──────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Fetching at a flat 16/9 and displaying at up to 5:1 meant the browser threw most
 * of the file away — measured at 64% discarded on a real hero at a 1890px
 * viewport, because `object-fit: cover` scales the file 1.5x to fill the width and
 * crops the overflow.
 *
 * So the rungs are HEIGHT-CAPPED (`capHeight` in image.ts): each candidate comes
 * back already close to the shape its box will have, instead of tall and mostly
 * cropped. Same URLs, same breakpoint count — which is to say none — and roughly
 * half the bytes on the widest rungs.
 *
 * The trade it accepts is that rungs are no longer all the same shape. That is
 * safe HERE and nowhere else: this box is fixed by `aspect-ratio` and
 * `max-block-size` without consulting the file, and `object-fit: cover` absorbs
 * whatever arrives, so a candidate swap on resize cannot move the page. The same
 * option on <SanityImage> would reintroduce layout shift.
 *
 * ── IT WILL UPSCALE ON WIDE SCREENS, AND THAT IS INTENDED ────────────────────
 *
 * The srcset ladder stops at the source's own width, so a 1262px-wide asset offers
 * nothing above 1262w — and this element is full bleed, so on a 1890px viewport
 * the browser stretches that file to 1890 and the result is soft. Confirmed in
 * DevTools: rendered 1890x384 from an intrinsic 1262x710.
 *
 * THAT IS THE DESIGN, not a failure of the cap. Full bleed is not negotiable, so
 * the only question is which way to be wrong when a source is too small, and
 * stretching the largest REAL file is strictly better than asking Sanity to
 * upscale it first: identical on screen, and fewer bytes. The cap governs what is
 * requested; the CSS governs what is filled. They are not in tension.
 *
 * What it does buy is an authoring responsibility, Andy's by his own call
 * (2026-08-25): HERO SOURCES SHOULD BE BIG. Roughly 2880px wide covers both a
 * 2560px display at 1x and a 1440px laptop at 2x. Below that, wide screens get a
 * soft hero — gracefully, but visibly.
 *
 * ── LOADING, AND WHY IT INVERTS SanityImage's DEFAULTS ───────────────────────
 *
 * `sizes` is a flat `100vw` with no `auto`: this is full bleed, so its box IS the
 * viewport and a measured value could tell the browser nothing it does not
 * already know. `auto` would be invalid here regardless — it requires a lazy
 * image, and this one must not be.
 *
 * EAGER, against the default, with `fetchpriority="high"`. This is the largest
 * element above the fold on an article page, which makes it the Largest
 * Contentful Paint — lazy-loading it would defer the one image that metric is
 * measuring, and the priority hint moves it ahead of everything else competing
 * for the first connections.
 *
 * ── PLACEMENT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * `inline-size: 100%` fills whatever contains it, so this belongs in the PAGE
 * STACK, never inside <Grid> — a grid would inset it to the content columns and
 * the full bleed would quietly disappear.
 *
 * At lg the hero swaps places with the page title. That is a reorder of the page
 * stack rather than anything this component knows about, and it is the page
 * template's job.
 */
import {imageAttrs, type SanityImageSource} from '../sanity/image'

interface Props {
  image: SanityImageSource
  alt?: string
  /** The shape at narrow widths, before the height cap takes over. */
  aspect?: string
  class?: string
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {image, alt, aspect = '16/9', class: className, ...rest}: Props = Astro.props

/**
 * 24rem at the nominal 16px root, DOUBLED for density — see `capHeight` in
 * image.ts. This is the one number shared between the frontmatter and the
 * stylesheet below, and they have to move together; there is no way to read a CSS
 * value from here, and computing the CSS from this would put layout in JS.
 *
 * It is a hedge, not a measurement: a reader who has scaled their text up makes
 * `24rem` larger than 384px, and the cap does not follow. The failure is a
 * slightly soft hero for that reader, which is the same failure an undersized
 * source already produces.
 */
const HERO_MAX_BLOCK_PX = 24 * 16
const attrs = imageAttrs(image, {aspect, capHeight: HERO_MAX_BLOCK_PX * 2})

/**
 * THE HOTSPOT HAS TO WORK TWICE, because this image is cropped twice: once by
 * Sanity, to the 16/9 file, and again by `object-fit: cover` as the box widens
 * past it. The first is handled in the URL builder; this is the second.
 *
 * `object-position` takes the hotspot's centre, so the region an editor marked
 * stays framed as the panorama tightens. Without it the browser crops to the
 * middle and a subject that is not centred slides out of shot on wide screens —
 * silently, and only for some viewers.
 *
 * A custom property rather than `object-position` inline, so the property name and
 * its default stay in the stylesheet and only the DATA arrives from the document.
 */
const focus = image?.hotspot
  ? `--hero-focus: ${(image.hotspot.x * 100).toFixed(2)}% ${(image.hotspot.y * 100).toFixed(2)}%`
  : undefined

const resolvedAlt = alt ?? image?.altText ?? ''
if (alt === undefined && !image?.altText && attrs) {
  console.warn(`[SanityHero] no altText on ${image?.asset?._ref} — rendering alt=""`)
}
---

{
  attrs && (
    <img
      class:list={['hero', className]}
      src={attrs.src}
      srcset={attrs.srcset}
      sizes="100vw"
      width={attrs.width}
      height={attrs.height}
      alt={resolvedAlt}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
      decoding="async"
      style={focus}
      {...rest}
    />
  )
}

<style>
  .hero {
    inline-size: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;

    /* 24rem = 384px. Component-local rather than a token: one consumer, and
       DESIGN.md names no chrome height. In rem so it tracks a reader's text size,
       like the rest of the type-derived spacing.

       KEEP IN STEP WITH `HERO_MAX_BLOCK_PX` in the frontmatter, which caps the
       srcset rungs against this number. */
    max-block-size: 24rem;

    /* Fills the box as it widens past 16/9. Without this the image would letterbox
       or distort once the height caps. */
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: var(--hero-focus, 50% 50%);

    /* base.css sets `height: auto` on every image, which is what lets the
       width/height attributes reserve a box from their ratio. Here the CSS
       determines the box outright, so this component cannot shift the page at
       all — the attributes are belt to its braces rather than the mechanism. */
  }
</style>
